WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue {
  font-family: Arial;
  font-size: 6.94vh;
  color: rgba(255,255,255,1);
  background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.6);
}

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Hello, everyone.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Can you hear us clearly now?

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, at the back of the
room—is everything okay?

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay. Thanks for being here.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
My name is Hager Mabrouk.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
I have to be careful because I
have trouble with directions,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
So I need to make sure I'm on camera.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
So sometimes, you'll see me looking.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
My name is Hager Mabrouk; I'm a
somatic coach and yoga teacher,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
But I worked in IT for a long time,
including at Oracle.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
And I'm Raphaël Semeteys,
Tech Advocate and Architect

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:44.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Senior at Worldline.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
And a practitioner and yoga
teacher for a very, very long time.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, our topic today is how
to become or go back to being

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
human full-stack developers.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
As you might have guessed,
this isn't a technical presentation.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
I hope you already know that.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
A human full-stack developer
is a person who knows their

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
resources and is capable of using them
in an environmentally friendly way,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
that is, by respecting
its rhythm and its nature.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that applies to every
layer of its system,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
So it's still a little technical.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
In any case, an architect
will have a vision for this.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
As you can see, we're going
to use the metaphor of systems

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
techniques to make this
more concrete and more fun,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
But let's talk instead about the body,
breathing, and the nervous system.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, when it comes to these technical
systems—we all work in IT to some extent,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
We know we have to take care of them,
that we have to pamper them.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
In fact, we spend a good
part of our work time,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
or even more, if there's a good fit,
to design systems that will

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
stay healthy—habits that
will last a lifetime.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
We create architecture that
will stand the test of time,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
We monitor and manage technical debt.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
We spend a lot of time and energy on this.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
On the other hand,
there is one system that we don't

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
always pay as much attention to

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
and yet it's incredibly
important—it's ours.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, so it's not easy for us.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for me, a few years ago,
I was managing projects at the time

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
and suddenly I found
myself having panic attacks

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
extremely violent episodes that
lasted three to four hours.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
I had absolutely no idea
what was happening to me.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
I thought I was a pretty
calm person, a pretty,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'd say, in control, in command.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
I didn't get very upset
with customers or the teams.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And actually, I felt
completely helpless about it.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
I really felt like I had
lost the documentation.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know what was happening to me.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And it took me several
years to get over it.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that's how I got into
yoga and breathing techniques.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
But actually, I realized
that, like many others,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:43.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Like a lot of people, I didn't listen
to myself; I didn't know myself.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
And besides, this is a trend
we see in the various symptoms

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
such as back pain.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
I imagine these are
things that some of you,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Unfortunately, they know.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Chronic stress, fatigue,
sleep problems, and burnout

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
which, incidentally, is quite
common among tech professionals.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's see how we can improve all of this.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
A quick disclaimer before we begin—it's
always required, especially in France.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're not doctors,
even though I've started studying

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
med school before doing prep school, but
that's not at all what we're going to do

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
go into the medical field.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
The goal is to share with you
knowledge that is well-established

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
and, above all,
plug-and-play tools that you can,

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
We hope you'll use it every day.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
To improve all of that, we're going to
use a framework that includes all three

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
layers of the stack,
the three pillars: the body,

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Physiology today, because
we only have 50 minutes,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
For us, too, they didn't want
to do three hours, so we didn't

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
I can't talk about everything.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathing, and then the nervous system,
which is the highest level.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Keep in mind that these three layers
of the stack aren't really separate.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
They influence one another and are
in constant contact with each other

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
with one another, as we shall see.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, so an architect's
perspective on that.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
What is "full stack"?
These are the three stacks.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's the infrastructure—that's
the hardware.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:06.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
That is the physical body.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for the runtime,
here we're focusing more on data

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:10.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
flows—the entire exchange of data flows—

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
the dynamic part of it all.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to focus a lot on breathing.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then there's the orchestration
layer—that's really what

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:17.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
brings it all together,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:18.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
an overall view of the system.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we'll start with something
that's very important: observability.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
When it comes to technical systems
and observability, we agree that,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:27.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's essential.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
And yet, when it comes to us,
it's not quite that simple.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
We are very familiar with our external
senses, which allow us to see,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
to hear and feel the world around us,
but we've lost touch with

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
two senses that are very important:
proprioception and interoception.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
What is proprioception?

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what allows me to
feel my body in space.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what lets me know where
my hand is without looking at it.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
It also helps you feel the movement
and the wholeness of your body.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
And interoception is really about
being in tune with my own rhythms

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
internal factors, my condition—whether I'm
tired or not—and also the

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
state of my contractions

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
and relaxation.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:10.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, basically, in a way,
interoception is monitoring,

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
the observability of the internal system.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Proprioception is the
monitoring of the relationship

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
of this system with the outside world.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's start with an exercise.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if you've read the abstract,
but we had warned that we

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
was going to do some practice runs.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
But don't worry—these
are very simple practices

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
and accessible to everyone.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I invite you to just sit as you are,
in a comfortable position,

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
without changing much.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And if that's okay with you,
close your eyes.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
If that's not OK, don't close your eyes.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
You know what you want to do.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just tune in to your body
and your overall state.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Are you more of a calm
person or a restless one?

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Are you feeling energetic or tired?

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:04.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
And do you feel relaxed or tense?

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
And it can be a mix of
both, because often,

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
We live amid paradoxes.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:16.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Next, become aware of the front of
your body, the back of your body,

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
from the right, from the left,
from the top, and from the bottom.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
And see if it's uniform, smooth,
or if there are any separations

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
between the various parties.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:36.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Next, see if there are any areas of
your body that are particularly tense

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:41.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
and areas that are particularly laid-back.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now, focus on your breathing.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Is she more on the slow
side or the fast side?

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Is it stuck or does it move smoothly?

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And is she calm or restless, too?

00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Simply put, without trying to change.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then, once again,
reconnect with your entire body,

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
given that your situation may have changed
slightly without you doing anything.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, reconnect with the room,
the group, and open your eyes.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:17.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Take a snapshot of your inner state.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:23.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
In fact, compare it to
how it was just before.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then, as we do the
exercises and make progress,

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:28.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can take little snapshots—like,
"Hey, is there

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:29.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Has anything changed?

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now that we've verified that
we're logged in to our system,

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll keep going.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, that was the first step.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
How can you work on a system if
you don't know anything about it?

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
It really is that observability.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
You may have sensed certain
areas—places where you felt

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
relaxed, or the opposite—super
relaxed—all that.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what we're going to do now is,
once we've identified

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:51.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for the system and the scope,
we'll tackle this in two phases.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
A short period during which we'll be
rolling out patches and

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:55.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
emergency fixes to prevent

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
that the system is going haywire. You
can tell something's really wrong here.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
We need to do something right away.
We can't let production fall apart.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, later on,
we'll see how we can work more

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
focus on architecture and perform
refactoring over the long term.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:07.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's start by applying the patch.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll start by correcting our posture

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
slumped in the chair.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
I guess everyone knows this one when
they're sitting at the computer.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:20.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël does it very well.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Does that ring a bell?

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël, how does this pose feel?

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:29.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm currently debugging
something I wrote in Vibe.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Damn, now that I think about myself,

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:35.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
It hurts here, in my lower back.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
My neck too, because my
shoulders are really hunched in.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:41.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Besides, it's making it hard for me to
breathe; I'm having trouble breathing.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Since I have to stand up, it hurts here.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
And also, since I'm all
curled up, my stomach,

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
It is completely compressed.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
It doesn't help my
breathing or my digestion.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:54.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
And your shoulders—how are they?

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not feeling very well.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's not great.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:58.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to apply the first patch.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I wasn't expecting a room like this,
but we'll try to make it work

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Still, the patch in that room.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:07.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
What we're going to do is

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
We aren't refactoring yet,
but we're going to start

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
from space to our posture.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël and everyone else—if you'd like,
you can wrap things up

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
the torso from the waist up.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël is going to bend over
and let his arms hang down.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can just leave your
hands on the table.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
But bend it well—try to
really bend it, you know?

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just like that, starting from the waist.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't bend my hips (I'm not going
to start making too many movements),

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:34.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't bend at the hips;
I bend at the waist.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
I let my mind wander.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, you stand up very slowly,
straightening your torso

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
millimeter by millimeter

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
millimeter by millimeter,
letting your arms rise toward the sky.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let your arms rise toward the sky.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Don't change anything in the lower back.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raise your arms toward the sky, lengthen
your torso, and let your shoulders rise.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
And lift your shoulders as
if you were gripping a bar,

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then lower your arms—if you can—to your
sides without bumping into

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:05.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
the person next to you,

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:07.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
and relax your shoulders.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, it doesn't completely change your
posture, but it gives

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
you a little more space.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do you notice a slight
difference? Yes, or no?

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
That was the first
patch—the postural patch.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you were in Raphaël's shoes,
it would be much clearer.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Plus, if you're at the office,
you can really relax while leaning back

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
at the seat to check if your
seat is in the correct position.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because sometimes, we have seats
that we paid €900 for and that don't

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
are not doing well at all.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not going to get into that topic,
or else it'll take me all 50 minutes.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
In any case, I feel more in
tune—I don't know how to put it.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, I feel my breathing—I'm
not like that anymore.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:47.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm fine.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, maybe we'll be able
to work on the next layer.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:50.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
He's nice.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now we're going to work on breathing.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're not going to release patches for
everything, but we're going to

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:56.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
release a patch to slow things down

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:58.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
breathing and make it more refined.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because often, when we're stressed,
when there are bugs,

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
problems, coworkers who aren't
very nice—breathing becomes

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
It happens quickly, and you
don't necessarily notice it.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
The first breathing exercise is
to imagine that you have a straw

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
in your mouth and blow,

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:20.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
to exhale through the straw,
but very slowly—the most

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Take your time, once again.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then, inhale very gently through your
nose, as if you were sniffing something

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:29.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
the scent of a flower.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Without getting too carried away.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:35.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then, once again, exhale through
the straw with your mouth.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Slow down, breathe in through your nose.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Go along with it—it can
really make a difference.

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:48.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
And one last time,
exhale through the straw.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:54.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathe in gently through your nose.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, keep up the same rhythm
and exhale through your nose,

00:11:58.680 --> 00:11:59.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathe in through your nose.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:03.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
Trying to maintain that
gentle, slow exhalation.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:06.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we're going to stop.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for me, I've already felt

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:12.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Normally, you should do the breathing
exercises for at least three minutes,

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:13.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
so that it makes an impression.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do you notice a slight difference
in your breathing? Yes.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:18.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Snapshot. Great.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:19.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Snapshot.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:24.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, we're going to fix the
devices that are overheating.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:25.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yeah, things are heating
up. Ouch, ouch, ouch.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
We saw it when I was sitting
like that, right there.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
My neck—I have to compensate to look up,

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
So that kind of takes the
wind out of my sails—it hurts.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
My shoulders are hunched,
and I have pain all over that area.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:37.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And the screen.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:41.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
The screen, my eyes—I spend my
life watching what the AI is doing.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
No, I'm exaggerating.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Basically, we don't really use our eyes
much anymore; we don't focus

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:47.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
on things in the distance.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
And you know, the body—how it works,
the muscles that don't

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:50.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
If they aren't used, they atrophy.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's the same with the eyes.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:55.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's why we lose visual
acuity and eye mobility.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what can we do to work on this?

00:12:58.520 --> 00:12:59.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's launch Visual Studio.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we're going to focus on two
areas: the eyes and the neck.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:08.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for the eyes, my advice is very simple.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
You're going to rub your palms together.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:12.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
The idea is to heat the place.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can tell it's a little hot.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you wear glasses like
I do, you take them off

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
and you can place your palms at
the level of the palms' hollows

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:22.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
in front of your eyes, which you close.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
The idea here is just to take it easy.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let everything go—your
shoulders and everything.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just focus on the warmth
in the palms of your hands.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:33.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Without putting pressure on the eyes.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Without crushing anything.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:38.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:41.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Right now, what we're doing
is just taking a break.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:42.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's just a way to rest your eyes.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when you feel things starting to
heat up seriously during the day, you

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:47.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can take little breaks like that.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that's going to cause
the system to go haywire.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:51.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, it's better to avoid it.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can take your hands away and put
your glasses back on, if you have any.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:59.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
We worked on stretching just now. Now
we're going to do some weight training.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Since we've said that muscles atrophy.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:03.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's very simple.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:07.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are lots of exercises in eye yoga.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, we're going to do two of
them—just so you understand.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:11.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
At the end of the conference,
there is a QR code to download

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
a booklet with lots of exercises
that are explained in detail, etc.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:16.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now, what we're going
to do Even more than that.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Excuse me? Even more than what's shown.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:22.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
The idea here is: you don't move
your head; you only move your eyes.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, look to the right.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:28.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
You're looking carefully into each corner
like that, without moving your head.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
And on the left side.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:36.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Right, left, right, left, right.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:38.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when you bring it back,
it feels a little weird.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:41.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you start to feel a little
discomfort, that's a good sign.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
That means your muscles
are actually waking up.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:44.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
That means they needed them.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Second and final exercise—there are plenty
more—we're going to do it diagonally.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:50.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
There, it's in the upper right corner; we
still don't move our heads—it's

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:51.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
in the lower left corner.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:55.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
It might seem a little strange in an
open-plan office, but it's good for you.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:00.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Top right, top left,
top right, bottom left.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we're going to reverse it.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:08.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Top left, bottom right,
diagonally, top left, bottom

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:12.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
right, top left,
bottom right. There you go.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
You'll see—there are other exercises,
but this one is really good.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:17.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
We can also relearn how to focus
because when we're in front of

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:19.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
On the screen, we no longer focus.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
But all of that is explained in detail.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
We did some strength training; now
let's work on our necks a little, too.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
This time, we move our heads,
but we don't move our eyes anymore.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:33.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Look at me and, gently,
breathe in calmly without looking away

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:38.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Looking away from me, turn your head
slowly toward your right shoulder.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then inhale and bring your
head back to the center.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Very slowly, slowly.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You'll understand why
this is important later.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:50.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Exhale, keep looking at me, turn left.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:54.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Maybe to slow down a bit,
imagine that this is the end of your

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:55.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
the nose that leads the movement.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:57.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's where the movement comes from.

00:15:58.520 --> 00:15:59.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathe in.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:01.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
In the center.

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:03.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Exhale.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:05.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm watching you out of
the corner of my eye.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:11.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we'll be back.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:13.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's very important.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to explain why taking
things slowly is important.

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:18.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just that alone helps you relax a
little. And that's the same thing.

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:20.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
When you start saying,
"I can't do this anymore."

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Take a deep breath, rest your eyes,
do some strength training,

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:26.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
and stretch your neck.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
And you've seen that in this exercise,
we're doing this passively,

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Always keep your eyes open.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
We've now patched the first
two layers of the stack.

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to patch the final
layer—the nervous system.

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:40.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for the nervous
system—and this is important—

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's when there are no threats—to
tell him that we're safe.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:47.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because the brain's job,
basically, is to keep us alive.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:52.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it's still a pretty
archaic way of doing things,

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:54.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Not only that, but in part.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:58.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
And he's still trying to figure
out whether or not there's a threat

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:01.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
and whether or not he
needs to keep us safe.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, one way—and since we
always have plenty of problems—

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:08.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
throughout the day—with the systems,
with coworkers, with managers, etc.,

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:11.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Stress can become a sort
of never-ending cycle.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:13.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
But the thing is, there aren't any
truly life-threatening dangers.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:17.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it's important to send safety
messages saying: there's no

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:19.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are no lions, no tigers,
no cheetahs—that's fine,

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
My life is not in danger.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:26.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm going to show you part of an
exercise called "orientation,"

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
where we'll scan the outside
environment somewhere using

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
our senses to tell us
that everything is fine.

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:35.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's start with the view,
and I'd like to invite you to leave your

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:37.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
His gaze wandered around the room.

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:41.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
You'll see that, in fact,
there are shapes, textures,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
colors, even in a room like this one.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:48.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And let your gaze rest somewhere.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:55.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
And once again, let your gaze wander.

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:01.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
There might be some things you
didn't see when you arrived,

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:02.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
that you see now.

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:09.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let it settle once more,
then return to the center.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, let's focus on touch.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'd like to invite you to
feel your feet on the floor,

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:23.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
resting your hips on the chair—and
perhaps your back on the chair as well.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:27.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And feel the clothes—feel your clothes

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:29.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
and maybe the feel of
the air on your skin.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
In places where you can feel it.

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:38.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, we could go on with the other
senses, but we'll stop here.

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
We just did a scan to make
sure everything was okay.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:45.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's no danger, at least,
aside from the human killers who are there

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
had at first, but they left.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:50.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
No, that HTTP request returns a 200
status code—that's fine, it's OK.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:53.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
So there's no need to go into
crisis mode or reaction mode.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:54.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're doing great.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:56.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
And it works. It's worth doing.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's definitely worth doing.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:00.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, orientation.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:01.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:05.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
What we did there was: we saw it,
we applied patches, we observed,

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:06.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can tell things are
getting out of hand.

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
We can calm things down a bit—our eyes,
our nervous system,

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:11.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
our breathing, and so on.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:15.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
But what we want to do is refactor,

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
We want to conduct a root cause analysis.

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:18.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's ask ourselves:
Why are we so hyped up?

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:19.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Why is that?

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:20.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Why is breathing blocked?

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:23.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
What can I do in the long term to
change the architecture of this?

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay, that's what we're
going to look at now.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to go back over the layers,
but this time, we're going to

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:28.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
a slightly more in-depth analysis.

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:31.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to try to
make lasting improvements.

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:36.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll start with the body,
and we'll revisit our famous

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:37.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
shrimp pose.

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:41.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
I call it "office shrimp,"
which I eat myself, too,

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:43.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
By the way, don't worry.

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:45.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël, a little while ago,
you said you weren't feeling well.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:47.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I guess it's the same thing.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, I'm going to ask you to stand up.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:51.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, imagine you've been sitting
like that for three hours,

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:53.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
months, years.

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:55.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Stand up, be righteous, and don't
try to take control of yourself.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:57.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's not very pretty.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:02.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, what happens is that
the muscle chains shorten.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And actually, we're in a
state of continuous flexion.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
This isn't a joke; it's true.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:10.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
But if you do that, you don't have the
space, but it would be the same thing.

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:12.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
So now, if you stand up
straight, how do you look?

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:14.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
To help you get back on your feet.

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:19.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
He's exaggerating a little,
but only slightly when

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathing isn't any better.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
And how's your breathing?

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:23.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's still stuck.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:26.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, my lower back was hurting,
but I can tell that if I stay

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
After doing this for so long,
my middle back is starting to hurt, too.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:31.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's going up because
there's a dip forming.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:34.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
On the downhill, it gets really steep,
and that can really hurt your back,

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:38.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
Whereas the natural dip should be
lower; it should be closer to here.

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:41.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what do we do most of the time?

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's just that you don't realize it when
you find yourself in

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:43.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
a situation like that.

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:45.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
And what would you do to make it better?

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:47.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
If I want to position my back properly

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:51.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
I tilt my pelvis forward
to try to get rid of the

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
The well-known retroversion of the pelvis.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:54.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not breathing any better.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
This time, my stomach is
completely knotted up.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm sure you've been told before that to
avoid back pain,

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:00.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
you should tuck your stomach in.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's a form of compensation that is

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:06.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Sometimes it hurts less than
having my back like this,

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:07.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
But it's a trade-off.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can try doing that yourself.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
You'll see, your stomach will
tie itself in knots right away,

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:15.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Your shoulders hunch in,
and that can lead to various conditions,

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:16.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
including psychological ones.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:22.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, to fix that, we need to add
some length back to the chains

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:25.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
muscles, improve muscle tone,
and restore mobility in the

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
my hips, my spine—I'm not
going to go into that,

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's a little beyond the
scope of today's discussion.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's hard to do all that while sitting in
a chair; otherwise, we would have done it.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:35.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
But anyway, right now we
can't go out into the hallway.

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:37.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël told me it would
still look a little messy.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:41.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
But anyway, actually,
we end up making up for it

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:44.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
constant, and we're straying
far from the left-wing stance,

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:47.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
the upright posture—a
posture I can maintain

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:53.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
standing effortlessly, with my spine
maintaining its natural curves.

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:55.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's a long-term effort,
but it's one that all of us,

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:58.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can do it to some extent without
necessarily having perfect form,

00:21:58.480 --> 00:21:59.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
but maintain a comfortable posture.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:01.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, no one has perfect posture.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
We always have a shoulder to lean on.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:05.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for me, I know that one of my shoulders
is a little higher than the other.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:08.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's called a "signature
shape"; it's kind of like

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:12.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
But we can still adopt an eco-friendly
stance that gives us a sense of purpose.

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:16.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just for the record,
my postural yoga teacher, who is

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:18.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
a high priest,
a fanatic of self-assurance.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:20.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
At one point, he said to me,
“Listen, it’s simple,”

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you want to get back on
track with your computer work,

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:26.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
You have to work on the floor and
hold your meetings on the floor.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:30.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And your toilet—you'll replace
it with a squat toilet.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:33.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
At that point, I said, “That’s the
red line—we’re not going there.”

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:37.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
And as you can see,
we wear shoes barefoot.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:41.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what my postural yoga
teacher said, too: after a while,

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:43.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you don't wear shoes like that, you're
not coming over to my place anymore.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:46.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
But that's a bit extreme; there
are extremes just about everywhere.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, luckily, we don't have to do all that.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:50.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can reshape your
body through exercise.

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:52.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
And why?

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:54.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because the body is
more than just posture.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:57.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's not a monolithic
architecture, actually.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:01.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
It is a fluid architecture—a
biotensile architecture.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:05.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if any of you are
familiar with this word: biotensegrity.

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:07.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Who's been through this?

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:10.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay, at least you didn't
come here for nothing.

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
So a biotensile structure is a
rather fluid structure where we

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:18.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
It moves, and it stays in place through
a balance of tension and compression.

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:21.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that's how we stay in shape.

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:23.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
There is always a balance
of tension and compression.

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
There is a type of tissue that is
very important, called the fascia.

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:28.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
I imagine you've heard about it.

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:31.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
So the fascia is that white-ish
layer you see at the butcher's.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:36.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
At first, we thought it was pointless,
but as time went on, we realized

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
and since it was really important,
really important for the movement

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:41.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
and extremely important
for the body's health.

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:46.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I might very well have
a problem I feel like it's

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:49.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
a muscle problem or a joint problem.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:53.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Sometimes it is, but it could also
be that the fascia has become stiff.

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:57.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
The fascia, on the other hand,
is what holds the entire body together.

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:00.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's why sometimes,
when you have back pain, the cause

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Back pain—it starts in the foot.

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
And this isn't a joke—it
happens all the time.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:09.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
What's more, the fascia itself responds
well to certain types of movement.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:14.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because when you're slumped in a
chair like Raphael, the fascia,

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:18.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
It will become stiffer, more compact,
and it will limit our mobility.

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:19.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
But there is a solution.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:24.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
If we move slowly, in a spiral motion,

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:27.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are other types of movements that
are good for the fascia, but I don't

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:29.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not necessarily going
to talk about it today.

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:32.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
But if we move slowly,
in a spiral motion that starts from

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:36.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
From the core, you can work on the fascia
without having to achieve perfect posture.

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:39.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
I really like this image—it speaks to me,
with its monolithic quality and all.

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:43.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
This somewhat monolithic,
static view of architecture,

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's as if we were saying: We
have a puppet, but the puppet,

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:48.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
If no one is holding it up,
it won't stay standing—it will collapse.

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Whereas in the biotensile vision,
it really is all about the interplay

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:52.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
forces and compressions.

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:55.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, it's load balancing,
it's a microservice, and it's distributed.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:59.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
And in fact, it helps build
resilience and distribute the load.

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:02.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's exactly it. It
distributes the load inside.

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:03.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
of the body, and that's
what keeps us going.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
And as a result, it's more resilient and
better suited to the situation, precisely.

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:09.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's an architecture that's much more
adaptable, and that's

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:10.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
what we're going to see

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:12.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
with smart motion.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:16.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can adjust it without—as I keep
saying—having the perfect posture.

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
To give you an idea of what "smart
movement" is, let's take a look at

00:25:19.640 --> 00:25:20.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Try it once.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:25.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
I hope you're not getting tired of this,
but keep your elbows close to your body.

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
See how you can position your forearms.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:31.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do the best you can.

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:34.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Can't you just put your forearms
in front of you like that?

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:35.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, that's it.

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:38.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
And turn your palms toward
the table—this time,

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:43.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
then toward the sky,
toward the table, and toward the sky.

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:45.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, it's a pretty mechanical
movement—we agree on that.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:47.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
You move your forearm,
and nothing happens.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:50.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
So now Raphaël is going to
show it, because I have this

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:51.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
that mic, even though

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:54.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to do the same movement, but
we're going to let the movement play out.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:56.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
There, you don't have much room to move.

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:57.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, watch me—I'm doing the same movement,

00:25:57.960 --> 00:25:59.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
but I let my elbow move.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'll let that one slide.
I don't see it right now.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:05.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let your back move with this exercise.

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:06.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let the movement pass.

00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:11.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's the quality of the fascia that
will determine whether or not it allows

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:12.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
pass the move.

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:16.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I know, it's not something
you do very often

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:18.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
at a tech conference,
but there's a first time for everything.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:25.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, when you do
that—the way you're sitting—

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:30.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's not great, but when you make
a wave-like motion, you don't

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's not just about
moving bones and joints.

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:37.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll also work on the fascia
and improve our circulation.

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:42.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
This undulating motion
is a fundamental motion.

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:45.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to try it out.

00:26:45.560 --> 00:26:48.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do you see how you might be
able to put a little more

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:52.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
on the front of your chairs; let go
of the backrest for a few seconds.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:54.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Place your hands on your thighs.

00:26:56.840 --> 00:27:02.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël will do it while sitting,
and we'll let his torso lean forward

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:04.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
very, very, very slowly.

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:06.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
You've got the table.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:10.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
In any case, you can't go very far,
but let your upper body move forward.

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:12.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Go ahead, let your head rise.

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:15.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then take a short break.

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:20.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now, press down with your hands,
let your back arch backward and round out.

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:24.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
The head goes back in.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:26.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let your back round out naturally.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
The back doesn't just
do that—it does this.

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:32.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Take a short break.

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:38.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
And once again, we're going
to take the other route.

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:42.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll let the upper body—expanding the
chest—move forward,

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
and the head will rise.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:51.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Push down with your hands and let your
back arch backward, rounding it,

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:53.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
and tuck your head in.

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we'll head back to
the center, nice and easy.

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:04.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
That movement is a fundamental
one that will bring about

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:06.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
work on all the fascia in the body.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:09.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
All fascia specialists recommend
this particular movement

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:14.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
to restore elasticity and hydration
to the fascia, but in reality,

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:17.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can do it sitting down,
but you can also do it standing up.

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:20.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
By the way, at Riviera Dev,
if we have people coming,

00:28:20.440 --> 00:28:25.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to do a standing exercise that
involves a lot of undulating movements.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:27.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are other types of wave motion.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:31.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And there's another one I
call that smart movement.

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:36.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Of course, you can exercise;
you can keep exercising,

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:39.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
But be sure to incorporate this type of
movement into your daily routine as well.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:43.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, we're also working on the spine.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:45.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
The spine is the pillar of our body.

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:50.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
If someone asks you, “Should I cut off
your arm, your leg, or your spine?”

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:55.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's better to keep the column because,
really, without the column,

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:56.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's nothing we can do.

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:58.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
And the column is really put
to the test in that position

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:00.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
slumped in the chair.

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:02.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it's important to help
him regain his mobility.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:07.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this case, we observed the
stretching motion of the spine.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here are the five movements of the column.

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:15.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
The flexion and extension movement
we saw with the undulating motion

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:19.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we'll have the other two movements,
which are the lateralization movement

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:22.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
and rotational motion.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:25.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll try to make them in

00:29:26.160 --> 00:29:28.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I didn't want to try it earlier
because I thought to myself:

00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:29.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's going to stress me out too much.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:32.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not going to want to give the
presentation anymore because he

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
There isn't enough room. But
anyway, we'll try to do it.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:38.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Place your hands in front of your
chest with your palms facing forward.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:41.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Be really careful around your neighbor,
especially if you're tall

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:43.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
and that your neighbor is short.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:46.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël, maybe you could turn your back to
me because I'm going to be

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:47.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
standing in front of the mirror.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:53.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
And slowly let your left elbow,

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:56.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
the left elbow—so, the left
elbow—raise it toward the sky,

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:58.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
because everyone has to pitch
in equally, otherwise we'll

00:29:59.040 --> 00:29:59.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
