00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:26.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then, return to the center.

00:30:29.080 --> 00:30:30.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now we're going to do the other side.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, always be mindful of your neighbor.

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:36.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I lift my left elbow,
and as you can see, I make an arc.

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:38.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Right. Right, sorry.

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:41.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Don't do what I say—I'm making a bow.

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:46.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go—turn your head and
rest your left hand on the chair.

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:49.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let the right side
stretch out and open up.

00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:53.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Take a deep breath to experience
the scent even more fully.

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:55.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
The microphone is a disaster.

00:30:55.600 --> 00:30:57.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm moving around; the microphone is
moving all over the place,

00:30:57.720 --> 00:30:58.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
so bring it back to the center.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:00.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay.

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:02.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now, let's do the rotation.

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:04.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know how this will turn out.

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:06.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I'll show you the move.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:08.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, you'll see how
you can organize your life.

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:10.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Fortunately, there are
a few spots available.

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:14.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, Raphaël, let's turn
the bust to the right

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:18.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
and let your right hand rest on
the chair, and your left hand

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:20.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
on the inner thigh or knee.

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:24.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
And keep your pelvis facing forward—or
even turn it just a tiny bit

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:28.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Tilt your pelvis to the left,
but your head And relax.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then return to the center.

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:34.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Slowly. Slowly.

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:37.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Think about your fascia and do
the movement on the other side.

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:42.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Keep your pelvis facing forward.

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:43.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's as if

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:48.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Can you imagine? We're at Sunny Tech,
and you're at the beach,

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:50.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
You know, when you wring out a wet towel,

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:53.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are two fixed points,
and you turn in opposite directions.

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:54.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's exactly what we're doing right now.

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:56.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're currently stretching the spine.

00:31:56.600 --> 00:31:58.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's really good for your spine.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:00.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
It brings up flows that
otherwise wouldn't come up.

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:04.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Even if we do it slowly,
we still have to take it easy.

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:09.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
And if you do it on the floor very slowly,
it's great for the fascia.

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:11.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
So all these movements,

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:16.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
When done slowly,
they have a very strong effect

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:17.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
on the spine and on the fascia.

00:32:18.080 --> 00:32:24.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And finally, the last point I wanted
to share with you is that the brain,

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:28.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
He has a body map that he
updates more or less regularly.

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:31.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if you're aware of this.

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:33.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, sometimes, I might have

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:35.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
I may have broken my ankle five years ago,

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:40.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't have anything left,
and yet I can't move my ankle.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:41.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
And this is no joke.

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:46.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
These are studies conducted by
Thomas Hanna and Moshe Fandelkreis,

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:51.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Empirically, and now this has
been confirmed by modern science.

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:54.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because when something
happens in the brain,

00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:56.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
When it's injured, it can
become invisible to the brain,

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:00.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Either he continues to view it
as an area he needs to protect.

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:03.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we need to let her know that
she's here and that everything's okay.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:07.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
What you need to do is move
all your joints regularly.

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:10.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're not going to cover everything here,
because otherwise we'd never finish.

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:14.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
So move all your joints
regularly and gently

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:17.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
to tell the brain: it's
there, and it can move.

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:20.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
So there's no need to
protect it—everything's fine.

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:21.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's very important.

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:24.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
I suffered a very serious injury,
and it was because of that

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:26.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
experience that I succeeded.

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
to help me get over this pain that
had been going on for years without

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:31.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm managing to get by.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:32.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's it for the refactoring.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
That wraps up the refactoring of the
first layer of the infrastructure.

00:33:37.600 --> 00:33:40.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
All right, let's move on to the
runtime layer. I like this part.

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:42.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to focus on breathing.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:45.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what are the underlying
issues we have with breathing?

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:46.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are several.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
The first is respiratory capacity.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:50.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do we have enough volume?

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Or do we use all the air in
our lungs when we breathe?

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:58.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
The rib cage is there to protect us

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:01.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
the lungs and vital organs.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:03.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's a good thing she's here; otherwise,
things would be tough.

00:34:03.560 --> 00:34:07.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
But the problem is that if it's
too tight, it will also compress,

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
It will prevent this breathing movement.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:14.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, the exercises we've been doing for
the past little while—lateralization,

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:19.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Taking it slow, rehydrating
the fascia, and all that—that

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:23.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's already a step in that
direction—freeing up the chest and,

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:25.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
as a result, allowing the
breath to flow more freely.

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:28.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
We've already started
working on the runtime.

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:30.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's just like we said—all these
layers interact with each other.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:32.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
with one another. That's the first point.

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:35.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
The second point is
physiological breathing.

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:39.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Optimal physiological breathing
is that in which the system,

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:42.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
It is built and operates in
the most optimal way possible.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:44.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are two things here.

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're talking about the lungs
and gas exchange in the lungs.

00:34:47.680 --> 00:34:48.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are two things happening.

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:50.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here's one: breathe through your nose.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:54.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
That is normal, optimal
physiological breathing.

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:56.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Why? Because the nose acts as a firewall.

00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:57.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's actually the firewall.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:00.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
It will filter what enters the lungs.

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:03.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's why we have body hair,
eyelashes on the inside, and all that.

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:05.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
It cleans up—actually, it's the firewall.

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:08.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are IPTable rules and everything.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:14.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
It will also be used to humidify the air.

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:17.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And make sure to set it
to the right temperature.

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:21.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's really designed to ensure that the
air is in the best

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:22.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
possible condition to be

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
and then incorporated into the lungs.

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:27.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
It even produces nitric
oxide in the sinuses,

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:31.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
which is something that makes
it even easier to absorb oxygen.

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:32.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
So breathe through your nose by default.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:35.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Even though this isn't always
the case, in normal mode,

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:36.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what we do.

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:38.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Second aspect of optimal
physiological breathing,

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:40.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
It means breathing with your diaphragm.

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:41.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
The diaphragm—do you know what that is?

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:43.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's a kind of muscle that's located
below the lungs, right there.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:46.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
And it's actually the main
muscle involved in breathing.

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:50.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we don't use it much by
default because we're a little off.

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:54.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
As we've seen, we breathe mostly through
the top of our mouths, and on top of that,

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:54.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
we're a little blocked up.

00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:56.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what can we do?

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:59.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
First, before we get to
diaphragmatic breathing, maybe we'll

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:01.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Switch to abdominal breathing.

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:02.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Follow me, put both hands up.

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:03.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
You know all about that—we've
already told you about it.

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
You'll see. You'll find out right away.

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:08.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
If that's not the case,
place both hands on your stomach.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:12.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when you breathe in,
imagine your belly as if it were

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:14.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
a balloon that inflates and
pushes against your hands.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:18.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Slowly, without straining, calmly.

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:20.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're relaxed, we're letting our shoulders
drop and everything—we feel good.

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:24.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when you exhale, the balloon deflates.

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:25.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Once again, slowly.

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:30.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can really go overboard. Take a look.

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:34.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's too much. Have any of
you ever seen a sleeping baby?

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:36.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
When babies sleep, they breathe
like that—using their bellies.

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, the belly is swelling—you
can really see the balloon

00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:40.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
that inflates and deflates.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:42.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's a great breathing
exercise to help you calm down.

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:44.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you're stressed out or whatever,
basically, you're not breathing

00:36:44.760 --> 00:36:46.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
enough or you're breathing too much

00:36:46.120 --> 00:36:47.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
High levels of
oxygen—hyperoxygenation—cause

00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:49.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
a whole host of other problems,

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:51.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Take a deep breath and do it.

00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:52.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Even in a meeting, you can't tell.

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:54.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just like that, no big deal.

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:55.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
What are you doing? Nothing.

00:36:57.200 --> 00:36:59.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's not what we want,
because that's the breath for

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:01.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
to be really not relaxed,
like when you're trying to sleep.

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:03.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
We want optimal breathing—in other words,
we want to be relaxed,

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:06.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
But we want to be just as
“aware,” as Jean-Claude would say.

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:08.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
We want to be ready to take action.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:12.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
So at this point, we need to
engage the diaphragm again.

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:15.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's easy to do—just rest your
hands on it for 15 minutes.

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:18.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Thank you As for the ribs,
they're loose right now.

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:21.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now, this time, when you breathe in,
you imagine—and maybe you even feel—

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:24.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Otherwise, apply a little pressure so
that the ribs spread out to the sides.

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:27.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
So more along those lines.

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:29.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
On the sides.

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:31.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when you let go

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:33.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
It might be tiny—it doesn't matter.

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:36.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because that's what breathing
is—it goes in every direction.

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:37.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Again.

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:42.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm going to exaggerate on purpose.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:46.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just like gills—as if
you were a fish in water

00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:47.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
and you breathe.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:50.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can try exhaling through your mouth,
as a one-time exception,

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:51.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
by making the sound. OUUUUU OUUUUU

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:55.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Come on, let's go check
out the other rooms.

00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:56.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
The ribs—they'll go back in.

00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:59.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
And let yourself be inspired.

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:03.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
And let the air in without
making your belly too bloated.

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:06.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Generally, after doing it a few times,
you can really feel your ribs

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:09.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are plenty of techniques
for spreading the ribs apart.

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:12.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you'd like, you can call me,
and I'll show it to you.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:17.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you feel movement here,
it means your diaphragm is working.

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:20.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because the diaphragm is like
a pump located underneath

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:22.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
of the lungs and really
makes breathing easier.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:25.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when you activate the diaphragm,
which is really, as I said,

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:27.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
the main muscle involved in
breathing; we're closer to the

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:30.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
physiological breathing. It's
going to be much more effective.

00:38:30.120 --> 00:38:33.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
We've talked about physiological
breathing, but do you remember from class

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:36.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
in natural sciences, where they
say: basically, we take in oxygen

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:37.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
and then we emit CO₂.

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:40.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
So basically, CO₂ is like waste.

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:42.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
We just need to get rid of it.

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:44.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
CO₂ isn't good. Actually, no.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:46.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's called the Bohr effect: CO₂

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:49.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's really important for the
second part of the breath,

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:53.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
that is, to deliver oxygen in the
most efficient way possible throughout

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:55.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
the body, through the blood,
and enters the cells.

00:38:55.880 --> 00:38:58.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
And in fact, if there isn't
enough CO₂ in the blood,

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:01.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
This absorption by the cells—it
doesn't happen very well,

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:03.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
It isn't effective. That's
the famous Bohr effect.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:04.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay?

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:08.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, the thing is, to make your
breathing even more effective,

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:11.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
What's needed is tolerance to CO₂.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:14.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are lots of exercises we can't do,
especially since there

00:39:14.360 --> 00:39:15.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
are only 10 minutes left,

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:18.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
But you'll find instructions in the cheat
sheet on how to increase

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:19.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
this CO₂ tolerance.

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:22.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because if you have too much of it,
it's not good, but if you don't have any

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:23.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
If there isn't enough, that's no good.

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:24.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And it's just like
Jean Reno in *The Big Blue*,

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:27.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
For those who've seen it,
who want to get ready,

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:30.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
who does hyperoxygenation, and basically,
it stresses his nervous system,

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:31.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is not going well at all.

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:35.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
In fact, when people have tetany attacks,
they put a bag over their heads

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:37.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
so that they increase their
carbon dioxide emissions.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:38.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
They breathe in their own CO₂.

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:40.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, so things will calm down.

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:43.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that, in fact, brings
us to the nervous system.

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:46.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, the nervous system is
the last layer we're going to

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:48.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
refactor, but we've already
started doing that by refactoring

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:49.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
the body and breathing.

00:39:50.280 --> 00:39:54.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
If the body is tense, the nervous
system perceives a sense of insecurity.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:56.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathing, if it is not physiological,

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:59.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Same thing—he'll think it's unsafe.

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:03.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, if we improve it, we refactor it.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:05.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if that's the right way to
put it, but we're refactoring

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:06.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
the nervous system.

00:40:06.440 --> 00:40:09.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
And I'd like to set the record straight
about stress, because stress isn't

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:11.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's not a bug at all—it's a feature.

00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:13.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, when our prehistoric ancestors

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:18.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
and ran into some saber-toothed
tigers—thank goodness it was stressful

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:20.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
so they could escape; otherwise,
we wouldn't be here.

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:23.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Sunny Tech is said to be a
gathering of saber-toothed tigers.

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:28.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
But the problem is when the stress
becomes a constant source of stress.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:33.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
In other words, we have bugs,
we have problems, and we react as if there

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:34.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
There was a saber-toothed tiger.

00:40:34.360 --> 00:40:38.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
These are habits we pick up,
and they're hard to break.

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:41.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, in addition to everything we've
done so far, as for me, there are a few

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:43.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
something I do all the time.

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:45.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
He's one of our yoga
instructors—he's Australian,

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
whose name is Simon Borg-Olivier,
who has written books and all that.

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:51.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
I really recommend checking out
what he does, if you're curious,

00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:55.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you like yoga. He says you
have to change your habits.

00:40:56.120 --> 00:41:01.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
And since we unconsciously
tense up, we have to relearn

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:02.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
to consciously relax.

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:05.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
And so, we can move
certain parts of the body.

00:41:06.160 --> 00:41:09.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's all do this together
for a few seconds.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:13.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's just check to see if we
can move our fingers and toes.

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:16.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:41:16.680 --> 00:41:19.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
Make sure your abdomen is relaxed
and your pelvis is relaxed.

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:22.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Check to see if you can
move your shoulders,

00:41:24.320 --> 00:41:26.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
that you can move your neck and head.

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:28.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
And relax your whole head.

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:33.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Relax your eyes, relax your
forehead, relax your jaw

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:35.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
and relax your tongue.

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:38.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
You don't have to do what
Raph does, but really,

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:41.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's a habit we can get back into.

00:41:41.560 --> 00:41:43.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for me, I really did it for myself.

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:46.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
It was easy to stay calm
when you're in a yoga studio.

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:51.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
What's important is being able to
stay calm when faced with a stressor.

00:41:51.840 --> 00:41:54.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
And really, getting into the habit of,

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:59.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
It helps you get back into that automatic
state of relaxation, just like you used to

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:01.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
the automatic contraction.

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:06.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Especially since when you do that,
when you manage to relax

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:10.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
In complicated situations,
the brain will tell itself

00:42:10.080 --> 00:42:12.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
that we have resources. So,
what is stress?

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:14.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Stress is all in the brain

00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:19.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know about you, but for me,
it's just an acquaintance

00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:20.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
which has really changed my life.

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:24.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Stress is all in the brain;
it compares the stressor to

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:27.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
on our ability to cope with the stressor.

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:30.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, if I were to come across
that famous saber-toothed tiger,

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:33.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Of course, I'm going to be stressed.

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:37.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
If it's Superman who runs into the
saber-toothed tiger, he doesn't care.

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:38.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Oh, it's a cat.

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:40.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
With really ugly teeth.

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:42.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do you see that?

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:45.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
If I get used to calming myself
down in stressful situations,

00:42:45.560 --> 00:42:47.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
The same stressor will stress me out less,
and as a result,

00:42:47.800 --> 00:42:48.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
things will go much better.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:52.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And the last point is that sometimes we
get stressed out because, inevitably,

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:55.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Either we couldn't help ourselves,
or there really is a real danger.

00:42:55.960 --> 00:42:56.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Well, I guess I really have to stress out.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:42:59.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
What we need to do next is calm down.

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:01.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
You shouldn't stay in a
constant state of stress.

00:43:01.680 --> 00:43:03.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Do you see any wild animals? I don't know,

00:43:03.800 --> 00:43:07.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
gazelles or antelopes,
when they are fleeing from lions,

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:11.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
or rather, the lionesses—they run,
and as soon as there are no more

00:43:11.800 --> 00:43:14.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
When they sense danger,
they usually tremble a little.

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:16.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, five minutes later—or
a few minutes later—they're

00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:18.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
to graze and drink without any trouble.

00:43:18.760 --> 00:43:20.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
We've kind of lost that ability.

00:43:21.120 --> 00:43:24.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Maybe you have it, but I,
at least, and a lot of people I

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:25.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
As far as I know,
they don't have that ability.

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:29.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And this ability—to put
it simply—is linked to,

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:31.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
to one called the vagus nerve.

00:43:31.840 --> 00:43:35.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
The vagus nerve runs all
over this area of the body.

00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:40.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
It is associated with the stomach, the
lungs, and the organs that generate heat

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:40.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
that we saw earlier.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:45.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
If it's centered around him,
80% of the messages he sends are

00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:47.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
messages that travel from
the body to the brain.

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:50.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is a relatively recent discovery.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:53.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
We used to think there
were more messages going

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:55.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
from the brain to the body. This is
not the case with the vagus nerve.

00:43:55.800 --> 00:43:58.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
If everything around him
is tight, what happens?

00:43:58.720 --> 00:44:00.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
He sends messages that
make people feel insecure.

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:03.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
But it has to work properly.

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:06.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
It has to work properly so
we can get back to normal.

00:44:06.280 --> 00:44:08.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is what's known as the vagal brake.

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:10.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
For that, there are now
electrical stimulators.

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:11.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for me, I've never used it.

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:18.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
But there are also some yoga techniques
that are very effective, such as

00:44:18.080 --> 00:44:21.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
to hum, to go "HUMMMMM."

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:25.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you do that for several minutes,
you might not really

00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:27.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Disturb the people in the next room. Sing.

00:44:28.200 --> 00:44:30.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
La, la, la, la, la, And then

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:33.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël does this every
morning—he gargles.

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:36.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's really great for the vagus nerve.

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:39.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are also quite a few breathing
exercises that are beneficial

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:40.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
for the vagus nerve.

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:41.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
In that case, we won't have

00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:44.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yeah, we're going to do it anyway.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:47.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'd like to offer you—as a one-time
exception—the chance to do one last

00:44:47.840 --> 00:44:51.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Breathe through your mouth
and exhale very slowly

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:53.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
and very slightly through the mouth.

00:44:56.240 --> 00:44:58.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
But finish exhaling through your nose.

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:03.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
You start the exhalation through your
mouth and finish it through your nose,

00:45:03.520 --> 00:45:06.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then you breathe in through
your nose. Through your nose?

00:45:06.200 --> 00:45:06.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Through the nose.

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:11.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
And once again, exhale very
slowly through your mouth

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:16.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
and finish exhaling through your nose.

00:45:19.720 --> 00:45:20.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:24.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is just one breathing exercise among
many, but it's well known

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:25.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
for its invigorating effects,

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:27.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
In any case, improve the
functioning of the vagus nerve.

00:45:28.200 --> 00:45:28.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:31.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're nearing the end—it's time to
put all of this into production.

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:34.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll work all of that in.

00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:37.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to do CI/CD,
but CD stands for Continuous Relaxation.

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:42.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
As we've seen, observation is part
of the Continuous Détente cycle.

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:45.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
You need to know what state
you're in to be able to act on it.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:48.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
If it's urgent, if you need to apply a
patch, you've been given some techniques,

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:50.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
We've seen how to keep things
from getting out of hand.

00:45:51.040 --> 00:45:54.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
And we also want to be able to refactor
and work toward long-term goals

00:45:54.120 --> 00:45:55.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
to avoid having to constantly apply
patches and end up with a

00:45:55.600 --> 00:45:56.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
production environment that is

00:45:56.440 --> 00:45:57.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Still cooler, though.

00:45:57.840 --> 00:45:59.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's one thing that's
important when you're refactoring,

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:02.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's the same as when you
redesign a system's architecture,

00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:05.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're not going to do that right away in
production, especially during a crisis.

00:46:06.640 --> 00:46:08.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's not when you're in the middle of a
major incident that you're going

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:08.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
to think to yourself: "Well,

00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:09.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm going to think about the architecture.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:13.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
The idea is to do that during
pre-production, not during production.

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:15.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, we're going to do this while we're in
the middle of a

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:15.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
crisis—that's not the point

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:17.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
that we're going to start training again.

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:20.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Oh, I saw a presentation at
Sunny Tech; he gave me some

00:46:20.480 --> 00:46:23.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
Right now, we're in panic
mode—there are tigers hunting us.

00:46:24.080 --> 00:46:26.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
As for me, that's what I
did with my panic attacks.

00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:28.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Remember, at the beginning,
I told you that I'd had some episodes

00:46:28.080 --> 00:46:30.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
of panic, and that's how
I started doing yoga,

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:31.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
breathing techniques.

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:33.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
The techniques were really good.

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:35.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Except that I wasn't training.

00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:37.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
I used them during the crisis.

00:46:37.760 --> 00:46:40.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
It never worked because
my system was overloaded.

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:43.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
I wasn't familiar enough
with them to set them up.

00:46:43.240 --> 00:46:46.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
When I started training—not
very long ago, 10,

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:49.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
15 minutes a day—it's
become second nature,

00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:53.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
putting these breathing
techniques into practice.

00:46:54.080 --> 00:46:56.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
I also improved my posture
and managed to get through it.

00:46:57.040 --> 00:46:59.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, just to be clear, I'm not
saying I know how to treat people

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:02.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Everyone has panic attacks,
but in any case, it might work,

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:04.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
This type of technique can be used for
quite a few different

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:05.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
types of panic attacks.

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:08.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, actually, it's consistency
that builds resilience.

00:47:08.320 --> 00:47:09.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, in a way, we're in
the process of reconfiguring

00:47:09.920 --> 00:47:12.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
a system, and we're
currently recompiling it.

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:14.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
For those of you who work with Rust—you
know what I mean—it takes time.

00:47:14.360 --> 00:47:16.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Compiling is a pain—it
compiles and all that,

00:47:16.760 --> 00:47:18.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
but once it's compiled,
you have the executable,

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:21.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
We can include it in the continuous
integration pipeline; it can

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:22.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
become the new norm.

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:24.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You need to reconfigure your system.

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:28.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
But if you're able to do that—to
observe yourself, to apply patches—

00:47:28.480 --> 00:47:31.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
when it's truly necessary,
and to take a long-term approach

00:47:31.320 --> 00:47:33.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
to recompile or reconfigure your system,

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:35.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
You have everything you need
to become full-stack humans,

00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:39.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
to become full-stack humans
again—since we already are, in reality.

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:43.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'd say that if there's one message to
take away from this conference,

00:47:43.600 --> 00:47:45.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
it's that we should take care of our

00:47:45.360 --> 00:47:49.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
systems—at least as much
as we take care of them

00:47:49.240 --> 00:47:52.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
that we create, because
our system, after all,

00:47:52.880 --> 00:47:55.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
It is the most important and
critical system we will have

00:47:55.920 --> 00:47:58.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
something that can never
be sustained in this life.

00:47:58.760 --> 00:48:01.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Thank you very much for your attention.

